The Day 244 (20:27) assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md and committed as bc635283. I had to use git add -f, because session_plan/ is gitignored and the plain git add from the instructions fails.

State: HEAD is 2f5f4abc. The harness ran the full test suite at session start and it passed; I didn't re-run it. The binary's -p test returned PONG. The last 10 sessions each finished 2 of 2 tasks with no reverts, and CI has been green for about 2 days.

Two size-gate cliffs: a task that edits either file could push it over its size cap, which fails cargo test and reverts the whole task.
• src/config.rs can grow by 11 more lines before that happens.
• src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs has no entry in the size gate's exemption list and can grow by 14 more lines.

Three new findings, all checked at HEAD:
• #1086 is still open for 8 shell commands. grep, docs, ast, def, outline, blame, lint and skill still print in-session slash syntax (/grep, /docs and so on) in their usage and error messages. Their exit codes are already correct; only the wording is wrong.
• Auto-memory has been stuck since 2026-08-12, with no ticket. MAX_AUTO_MEMORIES = 20, and once full, the auto-save path keeps the oldest entries and refuses new ones. All 20 slots hold watch-fix notes whose stored text is just "a test panicked at file:line", with nothing useful after it. Those 20 lines go into every system prompt. A product user with auto-watch on would hit the same thing.
• Some default models look stale (part of #1037). For openai, default_model_for_provider still returns gpt-6-sol, even though yoyo already has a preset for gpt-6.1-sol, which is Codex's default. The xai default grok-4 and the openrouter default claude-sonnet-4-6 may also be stale; I haven't checked those.

Research: yopedia (my research notes) already covered Claude Code up to 2.1.314 and Codex up to 0.166, so I saved nothing new. I checked two recent Claude Code fixes against yoyo, and yoyo already does the same thing:
• json and stream-json output escapes control and format characters.
• The partial-read write guard covers overwriting a file after reading only part of it.

That leaves #1094 item 1 (overwriting a file that was never read) as the remaining difference.

Waiting on @yuanhao, so not for the loop: #991 item 3, where I posted an either/or and he hasn't answered, and #1096, the X outreach challenge.

